## B) Attribute extraction  (be evidence-based about it)
================================================================================

Make extraction strategy choices based on measured performance AND cost
($ on LLM use). Stripping redundant text, fewer/shorter prompts, combined calls
are all levers to evaluate — not assume.

Test sets & evaluation:
- [ ] Robust, human-labelled test sets for ALL nine attributes (we only have
      civility + veracity scoring sets wired into evaluate.py).
- [ ] No overlap between test-set statements and prompt few-shot examples.
- [ ] Plot-accuracy utils; report MAE/RMSE/Pearson + cost per strategy.
- [ ] Hyperparameter / model / approach sweep (Opus vs Haiku vs Sonnet; combined
      vs per-attribute; with vs without few-shot; trimmed vs full input).
- [ ] Few-shot ablation: does each prompt's `Examples` block actually improve
      scoring accuracy? Combined extractor strips them (extract._condense_prompt)
      to save tokens; blocks preserved in prompts/fewshot_examples.md (+ inline in
      prompts/*.txt). Test full vs condensed on the scoring testsets. NB leakage:
      some prompt examples may overlap the testsets.

Sampling / selection bias (the score is the mean of statements the LLM CHOSE to
extract, not a representative sample):
- [ ] We evaluate SCORING (pre-isolated statements vs gold) but never SELECTION
      (which statements get pulled). Build a selection eval: hand-label all
      attribute-relevant statements in a few articles, measure extractor
      recall/precision.
- [ ] Missing-middle/salience bias: LLM extracts notable statements, so
      mean(extracted) != mean(all said). Score-all-then-sample to estimate skew.
- [ ] Source-mix confound: press releases (curated) vs Hansard (adversarial) vs
      RNZ (newsworthy) systematically shift scores → cross-politician comparison
      partly measures WHERE we have data. Report/normalise by source mix.
- [ ] Separate frequency from severity (current mean-of-flagged conflates them);
      needs the full denominator (rate × severity).
- [ ] Surface per-attribute n + confidence band; discount/flag thin (n=1) scores.

Attribute-specific methodologies (food for thought):
- [ ] Promises/predictions/factual claims (Strength, Divination, Veracity) can't
      be scored from text alone → build a longitudinal CLAIMS LEDGER: extract +
      structure (testable proposition, type, date made, falsification condition,
      expected resolution date). A separate verification pass (human now, automated
      later) resolves each true/false/partial with evidence. Score = fraction of
      RESOLVED claims that held true (real ground truth, not LLM guess). Also
      defuses the "calling X a liar" legal worry (evidenced verdict, not assertion).
      Seeds: integrity.jsonl (contains_claim), evasion.jsonl, promises.txt.
- [ ] Authenticity = consistency between RHETORIC and RECORD, not a per-statement
      score. Build two corpora per politician (public rhetoric vs parliamentary
      voting/division records + Hansard stances) and detect contradictions on the
      same topic. Needs Parliament division/voting data linked by topic.

